{
  "description": "Cross-language contract for the content screen (ADR-0077): the office's content terms and number detectors, found and masked. Python: aiq_agent.common.content_screen (tests/aiq_agent/common/test_content_screen.py). TypeScript: frontends/ui/src/lib/upload-screening/content-screen.ts (content-screen.spec.ts). For each case both assert the same matches (rule and matched text, in rule order then position: terms in list order, then iban, at_svnr, credit_card), the same masked text and the same findings, and that masking the masked text changes nothing. `matches` is a single pass over `text`; `masked` and `findings` are the result of masking to a fixpoint. Matched text, not offsets, is compared because Python counts code points and JavaScript UTF-16 units. `placeholders` is defined in Python (PLACEHOLDERS) and held here so the TS mirror cannot drift. There is ONE copy: the TS spec reads this file from disk at run time (vitest runs in frontends/ui with the whole repository checked out) rather than importing it, so the frontend typecheck never needs a path outside frontends/ui. `unicode` holds the character tables Python computed, which each side checks its own against for every code point: `whitespace` (the White_Space property, the only whitespace either side uses), `decimal_zeros` (the zero of every run of ten Nd digits; a digit's value is its distance from that zero), `classes_sha256` (per code point L, N, M or -, joined) and `fold_sha256` (the fold of each code point, joined by a line feed), all over every code point but the surrogates, made with Unicode `version`. `iban_lengths` is the IBAN registry both sides hold. When Python's Unicode version changes, the tables move with it: each is computed by its test in tests/aiq_agent/common/test_content_screen.py, whose failure prints the value to put here; then run the TS spec on a Node with the same Unicode version.",
